('p', 'Traffic density has three levels — none, defensive, aggressive — and defensive traffic applies courtesy rules (7 m minimum gap to stopped vehicles, no respawning near refuges) so evacuation-completion adjudication is not contaminated by NPC spawn artifacts.'),
('sec', '4.4 Pedestrian Dart-out Encounter Injector', 'ch4s4'),
('p', 'An independent process (hazard_injector) in the leaderboard container injects pedestrian dart-out crossings — a decoupled design that modifies neither agent nor runner. Encounter fairness was secured by repeated measurement: (i) spawning at the right lane edge (+0.5 m) eliminates non-encounters from outside-guardrail spawns; (ii) 1-s re-commanding and walking-speed verification (>1 m/s) block stationary mannequins; (iii) triggering by fixed route-point passage (settlement trigger in the dose-response experiment); (iv) ego-progress lead correction and stopping 12 m after crossing prevent side/late contacts; (v) ego position from the ROS localization topic (workaround for the actor-list-empty phenomenon, y-sign flip handled).'),
('p', 'The v5 injector adds three things: a state-establishment trigger that launches only once the compared state actually holds (lateral jitter ±0.2 m; preconditions verified by the manipulation check), fault-waveform seed fixing (FAULT_SEED), and physics-tick-synchronized recording of ego/pedestrian coordinates, speeds, and separation to per-case CSV.'),
('p', 'Injector reliability is managed as infrastructure: recording-channel survival is guaranteed by a watchdog (runs with lost records are invalid — Section 4.5), and the 8 m comparison arms the trigger only after the compared state is confirmed, preventing state-mismatched encounters.'),
('sec', '4.5 Collision Adjudication Methodology', 'ch4s5'),
('p', 'This section formally reports an adjudication-reliability incident and its reconstruction. The initial implementation adjudicated collisions with a collision sensor attached to the pedestrian, but measurement confirmed this sensor never fired in CARLA — silent even in cases where the official adjudicator confirmed a collision. A ROS-listener death additionally lost separation records, so many non-intervention-arm cases were misjudged as "avoided" without evidence; this was uncovered by video review, and all affected data were discarded and re-run.'),
('p', 'The reconstructed system has two layers. The primary ground truth is the real-time ledger of the ego collision sensor: the callback of the sensor.other.collision attached by the official adjudicator (CollisionTest) is patched to write, the moment a collision occurs, the counterpart actor identifier to a file (RAW-HIT ledger) — unlike official infraction tallies it survives early/forced termination, and endpoint-structure grazes are classified separately. Consistency audits comprise the pedestrian physics oracle (kinematic anomalies at collision — flung 12-28 m: speed >4.5 m/s or a >5 m jump within 1 s) and the separation stream (not fully independent, same physics engine; the initial 2 m jump threshold falsely flagged normal walking and was raised — stated).'),
('p', 'Verdict pitfalls of the runner exit labels: (i) the collision label may include endpoint-structure contacts (distinguish by ledger counterpart actor); (ii) the hazardous-stop label may include post-collision stops; (iii) official infractions are saved only on natural termination — a recording gap in an early-terminated case is not evidence of no collision. All encounter-level safety figures in this thesis are ledger-based.'),
('p', 'The clock for latency figures is unified to simulation time. Actor destruction at death injection stalls the simulator (~1.6 s) while the world stops with it, so simulation time is the correct clock and wall clock overestimates. Adjudicated latency is computed from injection and state-transition times written to append-only ledgers independent of case termination, with stop-before-destroy preventing residual frames from distorting silence onset.'),
('sec', '4.6 Distance Ladder and 8 m Encounter Experiment Design', 'ch4s6'),
('p', 'Spawn distance was varied as a ladder to measure the per-speed avoid/collide boundary. (i) Fault-free ladder: fault-free stock, {14, 11, 8, 6, 5} m × n=4 at a normal-driving (4.2 m/s) waypoint. (ii) Blinded ladder: stock under complete LiDAR degradation (lso-high), same ladder. (iii) 8 m encounter comparison: proposed low-speed MRM vs stock under gn-mid (EKF input contaminated only, perception intact) — cf excluded (signal reading impossible, encounters do not form) and gn-high excluded (mostly comfort stops); the selection process itself corroborates per-fault behavioral differences. (iv) Low-speed ladder: {8, 7, 6, 5} m at 3.0 m/s cruise (restart cruise) — launch protocols differ from the fault-free ladder, so the comparison axis is the encounter speed of constant-speed cruising (Section 5.5). (v) Dose-response experiment: after fixing the cap-delivery defect, caps {4.2, 3.6, 3.0} m/s × distances {6, 7, 8, 9} m × 10 seeds (S12-S21, per-seed randomness fixed — Table 4.3; gn-mid, injection at 25 s) = 120 runs. Launch uses the settlement trigger (spawn D m ahead only once the supervisor is in MRM_MOVE and measured speed has stayed in the cap-specific band for 1.0 s), guaranteeing an encounter under an actually holding cap at trigger level; the three arms differ only in the value on the identical interface (Section 5.6). Adjudication follows the ledger standard of Section 4.5 (the pre-ledger exception is stated in Section 5.3).'),
('sec', '4.7 Metrics and Statistics', 'ch4s7'),
('p', 'The primary encounter-level metric is the collision rate, reported with Wilson 95% confidence intervals. Inferential testing is pre-specified as the single primary endpoint of the dose-response experiment — the paired 4.2↔3.0 m/s collision difference at the 8 m spawn (exact McNemar) — with the remaining combinations reported only as boundary characterization (descriptive statistics with Wilson CIs; in small samples the exact McNemar p is discrete: 5:0 gives p=0.0625 and 6:0 first crosses 0.05 at p=0.03125). Fisher\'s exact test is used for unpaired auxiliary comparisons. As the manipulation check, a run with no launch within 420 s or a first-tick speed outside the band (±0.15 m/s) is rejected regardless of outcome and re-run under identical conditions. Exposure is defined as deg_dist, the distance from injection to reaching MRC.'),
('sec', '4.8 Reproducibility: Key Experimental Parameters', 'ch4s8'),
('p', 'Key parameters are summarized in Table 4.3, exactly as used in the experiments, with supporting sections indicated.'),
